      <div class="eyebrow">TRUTH ON TRIAL · FLAGSHIP CASE 000005 · POWERED BY TRUST-WORTHY</div>
      <h1>Did Jesus Really Rise From the Dead — Or Is There a Better Explanation?</h1>
      <p>Christianity rises or falls near this question. So no sermon, slogan, or skeptical shortcut gets a free pass. We will separate what the sources establish from what they imply—and from what only faith or philosophy can finally judge.</p>
      <div class="manifesto"><strong>FREE FINDING:</strong> Jesus' followers came to believe very early that God had raised him and that he had appeared to named individuals and groups. That belief is historical data. A bodily resurrection is one explanation of the data, not the data itself. Natural explanations face real gaps; a miracle conclusion carries a uniquely high burden and depends partly on whether divine action is allowed as a live possibility. <strong>YOU BE THE JUDGE.</strong></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="evidence">
    <div class="wrap grid">
      <article class="card">
        <div class="case-head">
          <div><div class="case-id"><?php echo $displayCaseId; ?></div><h2>The Claim on Trial</h2></div>
          <div class="status">OPEN TO CHALLENGE</div>
        </div>
        <p class="claim"><strong>Claim:</strong> After being executed, Jesus of Nazareth was bodily raised from death by God—not merely remembered, symbolically vindicated, or experienced in visions.</p>

        <h2>First, separate four different questions</h2>
        <div class="case-anatomy four-up">
          <div class="anatomy-item"><strong>Historical fact</strong><span>What the surviving sources directly establish about deaths, texts, claims, people, and communities.</span></div>
          <div class="anatomy-item"><strong>Historical inference</strong><span>Which reconstruction best explains those facts, with what confidence and assumptions.</span></div>
          <div class="anatomy-item"><strong>Philosophical judgment</strong><span>Whether testimony could ever justify belief in a unique divine act.</span></div>
          <div class="anatomy-item"><strong>Theological conclusion</strong><span>What resurrection would mean about God, Jesus, sin, salvation, and new creation.</span></div>
        </div>

        <div class="evidence-state proven">
          <h3>What is historically well supported</h3>
          <ul class="rules">
            <li><strong>Jesus was executed by crucifixion.</strong> That foundation was tested in <a href="/truth/case-000003.php">Case 000003</a> and is supported by early Christian and later non-Christian evidence.</li>
            <li><strong>Resurrection belief was early, not a medieval invention.</strong> In 1 Corinthians 15, an authentic letter generally dated to the 50s CE, Paul says he had previously “received” and passed on a tradition: Jesus died, was buried, was raised, and appeared to Cephas, the Twelve, more than five hundred, James, all the apostles, and Paul.</li>
            <li><strong>Paul personally claimed an encounter.</strong> Whatever caused it, the evidence is not merely that Paul heard a rumor. He placed his own experience in the appearance list and connected his reversal from persecutor to apostle with a revelation of Jesus.</li>
            <li><strong>Paul knew key people near the beginning.</strong> Galatians says he later met Cephas and James, “the Lord's brother.” That does not prove every appearance claim, but it shortens the chain between Paul and the Jerusalem leadership.</li>
            <li><strong>The Gospel tradition contains an empty-tomb claim.</strong> Mark 16:1–8, usually treated as the earliest surviving Gospel ending, has women discover an open, empty tomb and receive an announcement that Jesus was raised. Matthew, Luke, and John preserve expanded but varying accounts.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="evidence-state indicated">
          <h3>What is strongly indicated—but is still inference</h3>
          <ul class="rules">
            <li>At least some followers had experiences they interpreted as appearances of the risen Jesus. The sources are easier to explain if experience, not only abstract reflection, helped trigger the belief.</li>
            <li>The formula Paul says he “received” predates 1 Corinthians, probably by some meaningful period. The text does not stamp the formula with an exact date, so claims that it can be dated to a particular month or year go beyond the evidence.</li>
            <li>Paul likely understood resurrection as embodied transformation, not survival of a disembodied soul. Scholarly argument exists that this understanding presupposed an empty tomb; that is a semantic and cultural inference, not independent confirmation that witnesses inspected one.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="evidence-state unknown">
          <h3>What remains disputed or unknown</h3>
          <ul class="rules">
            <li>Whether Jesus received an identifiable tomb, whether that tomb was later found empty, and how much of the burial narrative is recoverable history rather than developed tradition.</li>
            <li>The nature, timing, and independence of the claimed appearances—including the claim involving “more than five hundred,” for whom Paul supplies no names, place, or independently surviving testimony.</li>
            <li>Whether the appearance narratives preserve substantially independent memories or later literary development from shared traditions.</li>
            <li>What any witness would have reported in an uninterpreted interview. Every surviving Christian account already gives the experiences meaning.</li>
            <li>Whether a divine act occurred. No surviving source lets us observe the transition from death to risen life.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h2>The strongest Christian case</h2>
        <div class="argument christian">
          <ol class="rules">
            <li><strong>The proclamation is unusually early.</strong> Paul preserves inherited material within roughly a generation of the crucifixion, not a legend first documented centuries later.</li>
            <li><strong>The evidence has more than one strand.</strong> Claimed appearances include individuals and groups; Paul claims his own unexpected experience; the Gospel tradition adds the empty tomb.</li>
            <li><strong>The message was specific.</strong> “Resurrection” in its Jewish and Pauline setting meant more than “his spirit lives on.” The claim was that God had acted bodily and ahead of the expected general resurrection.</li>
            <li><strong>Conversion and reversal need explanation.</strong> Paul changed from opponent to proclaimer. The sources also single out James. Grief alone does not neatly explain an opponent's experience, group claims, the bodily interpretation, and an empty tomb if the tomb tradition is historical.</li>
            <li><strong>No single natural theory covers everything cleanly.</strong> Vision theories may address experiences but not an empty tomb; body-removal theories may address a tomb but not sincere appearances. The Christian argues that resurrection unifies the data with less patchwork—if God exists and had reason to vindicate Jesus.</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="muted"><strong>Limit:</strong> Sincerity is not infallibility, transformation is not proof, and the force of this cumulative case depends heavily on disputed premises—especially the empty tomb and the degree of independence in the testimony.</p>
        </div>

        <h2>The strongest skeptical case</h2>
        <div class="argument skeptical">
          <ol class="rules">
            <li><strong>Our sources are committed believers.</strong> That does not make them false, but none is a neutral contemporary record of the event itself. The earliest detailed narratives were written later than Paul's letters.</li>
            <li><strong>Experience can be real while interpretation is mistaken.</strong> Bereavement experiences, visions, dreams, altered states, memory, expectation, and social reinforcement can produce sincere certainty without a returned body. Paul himself uses the same appearance list for earlier appearances and his later experience.</li>
            <li><strong>The accounts develop and vary.</strong> The narratives differ over which women came, what or whom they saw, where appearances occurred, the sequence of reports, and whether Jesus was immediately recognized or physically handled.</li>
            <li><strong>The empty tomb is not independently secured by Paul.</strong> Paul says Jesus was buried and raised but does not narrate women finding a vacated tomb. A bodily understanding may imply that Paul believed the body was no longer dead; it does not prove the later discovery story happened as told.</li>
            <li><strong>Extraordinary explanations need extraordinary support.</strong> People returning from irreversible death is outside ordinary experience. A mixed natural account—experiences plus later narrative development—can be historically untidy and still carry a higher prior probability than divine intervention.</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="muted"><strong>Limit:</strong> Labeling an experience a hallucination does not explain who experienced what, why the bodily-resurrection interpretation won, or what happened to the body. Low prior probability is not direct evidence that this event did not happen.</p>
        </div>

        <h2>Contradictions, tensions, and missing pieces</h2>
        <p>Variation is expected when several traditions survive, and some differences can be harmonized. But a possible harmony is not automatically the event's most probable reconstruction.</p>
        <ul class="rules tension-list">
          <li><strong>Visitors:</strong> Mark names Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James, and Salome; Matthew names two women; Luke names several; John initially focuses on Mary Magdalene.</li>
          <li><strong>Messengers:</strong> Mark has one young man inside; Matthew one angel whose descent is narrated; Luke two men; John two angels after Peter and the other disciple leave.</li>
          <li><strong>First response:</strong> Mark's earliest recoverable ending says the women fled and told no one because they were afraid. The other Gospels narrate reports to disciples and appearances.</li>
          <li><strong>Geography and sequence:</strong> Mark directs the disciples toward Galilee; Matthew emphasizes Galilee; Luke concentrates appearances in and around Jerusalem and tells the disciples to remain there; John includes both Jerusalem and Galilee.</li>
          <li><strong>Recognition and touch:</strong> Some stories emphasize eating or physical contact; others depict sudden appearances, disappearance, withheld recognition, or “do not hold on to me.”</li>
          <li><strong>Missing external record:</strong> No surviving Roman or Jewish document records inspecting the empty tomb or interviewing the alleged witnesses. Absence is not disproof, but it limits verification.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="explanations">Competing explanations on trial</h2>
        <div class="explanation-table" role="region" aria-label="Comparison of resurrection explanations" tabindex="0">
          <table>
            <caption class="sr-only">Comparison of leading explanations for the resurrection evidence</caption>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Explanation</th><th scope="col">What it explains well</th><th scope="col">Where it strains</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">God bodily raised Jesus</th><td data-label="Explains well">Early belief, embodied meaning, appearances, transformation, and—if historical—the empty tomb in one account.</td><td data-label="Where it strains">Invokes a unique divine cause; depends on disputed facts and a theological background not established by the reports alone.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Visions / bereavement experiences</th><td data-label="Explains well">Sincere appearance belief and some individual experiences without fraud.</td><td data-label="Where it strains">Group claims, Paul's opponent status, bodily interpretation, and the tomb require added explanation.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Body moved or tomb confused + experiences</th><td data-label="Explains well">Could combine an empty location with sincere experiences.</td><td data-label="Where it strains">No early source identifies a mover or corrected tomb; requires several events that happened to reinforce one conclusion.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Legend and literary development</th><td data-label="Explains well">Growth and variation from Paul's list to increasingly concrete Gospel scenes.</td><td data-label="Where it strains">The resurrection proclamation and claimed appearances are already early; development cannot be the whole origin story.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Deliberate fraud</th><td data-label="Explains well">Could manufacture a tomb claim or coordinated testimony.</td><td data-label="Where it strains">No positive evidence of a conspiracy; poorly explains Paul's reversal and the apparent sincerity of the movement.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Jesus survived crucifixion</th><td data-label="Explains well">Would produce later sightings without resurrection.</td><td data-label="Where it strains">Roman execution, severe trauma, and a wounded survivor do not naturally yield belief in transformed, death-defeating life. Evidence for survival is absent.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Mixed / presently indeterminate</th><td data-label="Explains well">Accepts that different data may have different causes and refuses false certainty.</td><td data-label="Where it strains">Can become an unfalsifiable basket of possibilities rather than a concrete reconstruction.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h2>Provisional Trust-Worthy finding</h2>
        <div class="finding verdict-list">
          <p><strong>Jesus' death by crucifixion:</strong> strongly supported.</p>
          <p><strong>Very early belief that God raised Jesus:</strong> strongly supported.</p>
          <p><strong>Followers had experiences interpreted as appearances:</strong> strongly indicated.</p>
          <p><strong>A historically empty tomb:</strong> credible but disputed; moderate confidence, not a minimum fact imposed on every reconstruction.</p>
          <p><strong>God bodily raised Jesus:</strong> a serious cumulative explanation, but not historically compelled in the way crucifixion and early belief are. The verdict turns partly on disputed evidence and partly on one's justified prior view of God and miracles.</p>
          <p><strong>Christian theology as a whole:</strong> not automatically proved. Even if resurrection is accepted, claims about incarnation, atonement, Trinity, Scripture, church authority, and salvation still require their own arguments.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="bobinated">
          <div class="eyebrow">BOBINATED OPINION · REASONED JUDGMENT, NOT PROOF</div>
          <h2>I do not think “they made it up” is good enough.</h2>
          <p>The evidence is too early and too rooted in claimed experience for casual dismissal. Something powerful happened that convinced Jesus' followers—and later Paul—that death had not won. I also do not think an unexplained experience becomes a proven miracle simply because every natural reconstruction has loose ends.</p>
          <p><strong>My judgment:</strong> bodily resurrection is the explanation I find most compelling when the evidence is read inside the possibility that God exists and acted through Jesus. I would call it warranted faith grounded in evidence, not laboratory proof or a conclusion history can force on every honest person. The empty tomb strengthens that judgment, but the early appearances and the kind of conviction they produced carry more weight for me than pretending every Gospel detail fits without tension.</p>
          <p class="muted">What could change this opinion: stronger evidence that the appearance traditions developed late, a persuasive natural reconstruction that explains Paul and the earliest proclamation without ad hoc additions, or evidence that the burial/empty-tomb tradition is demonstrably secondary. Stronger independent evidence for the tomb or the named witnesses would move it the other direction.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="judge" id="judge">YOU BE THE JUDGE.<small>WEIGH THE FACTS · TEST THE INFERENCE · NAME YOUR ASSUMPTIONS</small></div>
      </article>

      <aside class="card source-rail">
        <div class="eyebrow">EVIDENCE LABELS</div>
        <h2>Do not blend these.</h2>
        <p><span class="source-tag fact">FACT</span> A source exists, says something, or can be historically anchored.</p>
        <p><span class="source-tag inference">INFERENCE</span> The best explanation drawn from facts.</p>
        <p><span class="source-tag disputed">DISPUTED</span> Serious scholarship does not agree that the premise is established.</p>
        <p><span class="source-tag theology">THEOLOGY</span> Meaning assigned to an event through claims about God.</p>
        <div class="finding"><strong>Bottom line:</strong> The historical case gets us confidently to early resurrection belief and claimed experiences. Moving from “they believed they saw him” to “God raised him” is an inference with philosophical and theological content.</div>
        <h3>Read in sequence</h3>
