        <h2>Primary texts first. Scholarship second.</h2>
        <div class="source-grid">
          <section>
            <h3>Earliest and primary Christian texts</h3>
            <ul class="rules">
              <li><a href="https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=1+Corinthians+15%3A1-28&amp;version=NRSVUE" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>1 Corinthians 15:1–28</strong></a> — Paul's received tradition, appearance list, personal claim, and resurrection argument.</li>
              <li><a href="https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Galatians+1%3A11-24%3B2%3A1-10&amp;version=NRSVUE" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Galatians 1:11–2:10</strong></a> — Paul's claimed revelation and later contact with Cephas, James, and Jerusalem leaders.</li>
              <li><a href="https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Mark+15%3A33-16%3A8&amp;version=NRSVUE" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Mark 15:33–16:8</strong></a> — burial and the earliest recoverable Gospel empty-tomb ending.</li>
              <li><a href="https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Matthew+28%3BLuke+24%3BJohn+20-21&amp;version=NRSVUE" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Matthew 28; Luke 24; John 20–21</strong></a> — later canonical narratives to compare, not collapse into one account.</li>
            </ul>
          </section>
          <section>
            <h3>Scholarly and philosophical checks</h3>
            <ul class="rules">
              <li><a href="https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/new-testament-studies/article/resurrection-in-paganism-and-the-question-of-an-empty-tomb-in-1-corinthians-15/EF4DE640BE9104A454C7847ECF899313" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>John Granger Cook, New Testament Studies</strong></a> — argues from ancient language and concepts that Paul's resurrection claim presupposed an empty tomb, while explicitly distinguishing that from proof of the tomb narrative.</li>
              <li><a href="https://www.bloomsbury.com/us/resurrection-of-jesus-9780567697561/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Dale C. Allison Jr., The Resurrection of Jesus</strong></a> — broad historical-critical treatment of formulas, appearances, tomb traditions, parallels, apologetic overreach, skeptical overreach, and modest conclusions.</li>
              <li><a href="https://academic.oup.com/book/35151" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Matthew Levering, Did Jesus Rise from the Dead?</strong></a> — a clear affirmative Christian case combining historical and theological arguments.</li>
              <li><a href="https://academic.oup.com/book/1786/chapter/141456139" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Phillip H. Wiebe, “Christic Appearances and Visions”</strong></a> — surveys traditional, reductionist, and fideist interpretations and uses comparative visionary phenomena as relevant evidence.</li>
              <li><a href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11097-020-09666-2" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Matthew Ratcliffe, “Sensed Presence without Sensory Qualities”</strong></a> — an open-access study of common, cross-cultural bereavement experiences. It supports treating grief-related experiences as a live comparison, not as proof of what happened to Jesus' followers.</li>
              <li><a href="https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/scottish-journal-of-theology/article/on-the-historicity-of-jesus-resurrection/B4CD50745683F95144E7D388BBD663FF" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>John E. Thiel, “On the Historicity of Jesus' Resurrection”</strong></a> — a recent map of psychological, visionary, historical, and theological accounts that keeps the historicity of an experience distinct from the objective event believers infer.</li>
              <li><a href="https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/miracles/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Miracles”</strong></a> — maps Hume's testimonial challenge, replies, probability questions, and the philosophical burden behind miracle claims.</li>
              <li><a href="https://assets.cambridge.org/97811084/23700/excerpt/9781108423700_excerpt.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>The New Cambridge Companion to St Paul</strong></a> — chronology placing Paul's major letters in the 50s CE, before the canonical Gospels.</li>
            </ul>
          </section>
        </div>
